1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,061
Pilgrims in tall black hats.

2
00:00:04,361 --> 00:00:06,677
Native guests at a long table.

3
00:00:06,977 --> 00:00:09,546
A roast turkey, and pumpkin pie.

4
00:00:09,846 --> 00:00:12,871
This is the First
Thanksgiving, as millions

5
00:00:12,871 --> 00:00:15,679
of Americans picture it every November.

6
00:00:15,979 --> 00:00:19,959
But the only surviving
written eyewitness account

7
00:00:19,959 --> 00:00:24,105
of that feast, in 1621,
is a few lines in a letter.

8
00:00:24,405 --> 00:00:27,946
Those lines never call
the feast a thanksgiving.

9
00:00:28,246 --> 00:00:30,434
And they never mention a turkey.

10
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:39,623
In this episode, we read that letter,
follow how a harvest feast

11
00:00:39,623 --> 00:00:44,292
became "the First Thanksgiving,"
and look at a painting, made nearly

12
00:00:44,292 --> 00:00:49,030
three centuries later, that helped
give us the picture we know today.

13
00:00:55,367 --> 00:00:59,391
In December 1621, Edward
Winslow, one of the English

14
00:00:59,391 --> 00:01:04,047
settlers at Plymouth, wrote
a letter to a friend in England.

15
00:01:04,347 --> 00:01:07,016
It was printed in London the next year,

16
00:01:07,016 --> 00:01:10,095
in a small book now known
as Mourt's Relation.

17
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:18,682
After the harvest, he wrote,
the governor sent four men out fowling,

18
00:01:18,682 --> 00:01:24,017
so that they might rejoice together after
gathering the fruits of their labors.

19
00:01:24,317 --> 00:01:27,428
Many of the Native people came, among them

20
00:01:27,428 --> 00:01:31,279
their greatest king, Massasoit,
with some ninety men.

21
00:01:31,579 --> 00:01:35,468
For three days, the settlers
entertained and feasted them,

22
00:01:35,468 --> 00:01:38,469
and the guests went out
and killed five deer.

23
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,481
This short account tells us only so much.

24
00:01:45,781 --> 00:01:49,972
Fowl, which may have included
ducks, geese, or turkeys.

25
00:01:50,272 --> 00:01:53,153
Venison, brought by Massasoit's men.

26
00:01:53,453 --> 00:01:54,853
Three days.

27
00:01:55,011 --> 00:01:58,451
Massasoit and about ninety
men, and about fifty

28
00:01:58,451 --> 00:02:02,413
English settlers, the survivors
of their first winter.

29
00:02:05,867 --> 00:02:09,470
The colony's governor,
William Bradford, later wrote

30
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:13,427
that there was a great store
of wild turkeys that autumn.

31
00:02:13,727 --> 00:02:18,661
But that passage describes the season's
hunting, not the feast itself.

32
00:02:18,961 --> 00:02:23,630
And his history stayed
in manuscript until 1856.

33
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:31,787
For the settlers, a day
of thanksgiving was something else:

34
00:02:31,787 --> 00:02:36,943
a solemn religious day, set apart
to thank God for a particular blessing.

35
00:02:37,243 --> 00:02:43,725
In 1623, during a long drought, Plymouth
first held a day of fasting and prayer.

36
00:02:44,025 --> 00:02:45,425
Rain came.

37
00:02:45,582 --> 00:02:49,565
Later, they set apart another
day, a day of thanksgiving.

38
00:02:49,865 --> 00:02:55,803
Winslow's letter of 1621 does not
use that word for the harvest feast.

39
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:02,031
The feast also brought together allies.

40
00:03:02,331 --> 00:03:05,198
That spring, Massasoit
and the settlers had

41
00:03:05,198 --> 00:03:08,611
promised peace, and to help
each other if attacked.

42
00:03:08,911 --> 00:03:12,175
That autumn, the same
people shared one feast.

43
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,540
In 1841, the historian Alexander
Young reprinted Winslow's

44
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:27,398
letter in a book of Pilgrim
documents, and added a short note:

45
00:03:27,398 --> 00:03:32,813
this was the first Thanksgiving,
the harvest festival of New England.

46
00:03:33,113 --> 00:03:37,038
He added that they "no doubt"
feasted on wild turkey.

47
00:03:37,338 --> 00:03:39,793
The letter itself does not say so.

48
00:03:42,733 --> 00:03:46,515
Meanwhile, the magazine
editor Sarah Josepha Hale

49
00:03:46,515 --> 00:03:50,218
was asking for one national
day of Thanksgiving.

50
00:03:50,518 --> 00:03:54,384
In a letter to President
Abraham Lincoln in 1863,

51
00:03:54,384 --> 00:03:58,813
she wrote that she had urged
the idea for fifteen years.

52
00:03:59,113 --> 00:04:04,244
That October, in the middle of the Civil
War, Lincoln called on Americans

53
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:09,445
to keep the last Thursday of November
as a day of thanksgiving and praise.

54
00:04:09,745 --> 00:04:13,125
His proclamation does
not mention the Pilgrims.

55
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:17,161
Later still, painters
filled in the picture.

56
00:04:17,461 --> 00:04:21,633
In the early twentieth century,
one popular painting showed

57
00:04:21,633 --> 00:04:25,732
long tables, white collars,
and Native guests in feathered

58
00:04:25,732 --> 00:04:29,544
headdresses of the Great
Plains, far from New England.

59
00:04:29,844 --> 00:04:33,599
It was painted nearly three
centuries after the feast.

60
00:04:37,133 --> 00:04:40,088
Today, the day holds
more than one meaning.

61
00:04:40,388 --> 00:04:45,839
At Cole's Hill in Plymouth, the United
American Indians of New England

62
00:04:45,839 --> 00:04:51,053
has held a National Day of Mourning
on Thanksgiving Day since 1970.

63
00:04:53,567 --> 00:04:56,299
So what happened in 1621?

64
00:04:56,599 --> 00:05:00,567
A three-day harvest feast,
shared by English settlers

65
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:04,000
and Massasoit's people,
with fowl and venison.

66
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:07,957
The letter that records it does
not call it a thanksgiving.

67
00:05:08,257 --> 00:05:13,078
The name, the November date,
and the familiar picture were added later.

68
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:18,061
Every story has an origin.

69
00:05:18,361 --> 00:05:21,870
Sometimes, it begins
with a few lines in a letter.

70
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:27,959
This video is based on historical
texts and public museum collections.

71
00:05:28,259 --> 00:05:31,216
The narration uses synthetic speech,

72
00:05:31,216 --> 00:05:35,158
and some images are
AI-generated reconstructions.

73
00:05:35,458 --> 00:05:39,414
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

74
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:42,483
If you enjoyed this story, please like

75
00:05:42,483 --> 00:05:45,835
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

76
00:05:46,135 --> 00:05:47,907
Thank you for watching.
